{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields/reader-packet-1-settle.json'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields/reader-packet-1-settle.json'","aggregated_output":"{\n \"articles\": [\n  {\n   \"article\": 1,\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock is Historically Cheap. The Decade-Low Multiple Reflects Risks, But This Looks Like a Can't-Miss Buy Opportunity for NVDA.\",\n   \"publisher\": \"barchart.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-22\",\n   \"text\": \"Nvidia Stock is Historically Cheap. The Decade-Low Multiple Reflects Risks, But This Looks Like a Can't-Miss Buy Opportunity for NVDA.\\nNvidia (NVDA) stock is currently trading at less than 17 times expected forward 12-month earnings, representing the cheapest valuation the shares have commanded in over a decade and roughly half the multiple investors were willing to pay as recently as early 2025.\\nThis dramatic de-rating has occurred against a backdrop of accelerating, not decelerating, fundamentals — a paradox that sits at the heart of the debate over whether NVDA's compressed multiple signals heightened risk or a rare value buying opportunity.\\nSpaceX Has Huge, Unusual Put Options Volume - Is SPCX Set to Fall or Rise From Here?\\nLucid Just Struck a Partnership With Bolt on Mobility. What This Means for LCID Stock.\\nThe disconnect between Nvidia's soaring earnings power and its shrinking valuation multiple suggests the market is pricing in meaningful skepticism about the sustainability of the current AI spending cycle.\\nThe fundamental case for Nvidia remains extraordinarily strong. Fiscal second-quarter revenue more than doubled year over year to $96.2 billion, with data center revenue surging 117% to $89 billion, and management has guided for approximately $108 billion in third-quarter revenue, implying 89% growth.\\nCEO Jensen Huang has projected that chip sales could double in calendar year 2027, while CFO Colette Kress guided for 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — a figure that far exceeded the 45% Wall Street had previously modeled. There has never been a company of Nvidia's scale growing at anything close to this rate.\\nYet several legitimate risks explain the market's caution. Nvidia is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off its own balance sheet.\\nOther tangible headwinds include calls from leading artificial intelligence (AI) labs, including Anthropic and OpenAI, to slow frontier development; rising community backlash against data center construction; soaring interest rates that pressure hyperscaler capital budgets; and Nvidia's complete exclusion from the Chinese data center market.\\nAdditionally, off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects like the SB Energy campus for OpenAI introduce credit risk that traditional valuation metrics do not capture.\\nThe valuation math, however, is difficult to ignore. At 14 times next fiscal year's consensus earnings estimate, Nvidia trades at a fraction of its 10-year average price/earnings (P/E) ratio of 61 – and well below semiconductor peers like Advanced Micro Devices (AMD) at over 200 times trailing earnings and Broadcom (AVGO) at 46 times.\\nAnalysts remain overwhelmingly bullish, with 45 of 50 firms rating the stock a \\\"Strong Buy,\\\" a mean price target near $326 implying 42% upside from current levels, and earnings estimates that have been revised sharply higher — from $8.50 to $15.68 per share for fiscal 2029 — over just the past nine months.\\nIf Nvidia simply maintains its current trailing P/E of 28 while delivering on consensus earnings projections, the stock would roughly double over the next 12 to 18 months.\\nThe decade-low multiple is not so much a red flag as it is the market's attempt to reconcile unprecedented growth with unprecedented uncertainty about how long that growth can last. For investors with a multi-year horizon and a tolerance for volatility tied to AI spending cycles, the risk-reward profile at current levels tilts decisively toward opportunity rather than warning.\\nThis article was created with the support of automated content tools from our partners at Sigma.AI. Together, our financial data and AI solutions help us to deliver more informed market headline analysis to readers faster than ever.\",\n   \"named_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"figure\": 1,\n     \"text\": \"the 45% revenue growth expected for fiscal 2028 before the guide\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"figure\": 2,\n     \"text\": \"the 70% revenue growth guided for fiscal 2028\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"open_questions\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n     \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 2,\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia’s Immense Dividend Hike Puts Dividend Growth Stocks in the Spotlight\",\n   \"publisher\": \"morningstar.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"text\": \"Is Nvidia a True Dividend Growth Stock?\\nWhat’s the Big Deal About Dividend Growth Anyway?\\nWhy Are Dividend Growth Stocks Performing Well in 2026?\\nWhat Does Nvidia Joining the Dividend Growth Party Mean?\\nNvidia’s Immense Dividend Hike Puts Dividend Growth Stocks in the Spotlight\\nCompanies growing their dividends have posted strong returns this year. Why is that?\\nLost in a torrent of news about Nvidia NVDA this year—its role in financing $500 billion in artificial intelligence investments , its forecasts of 70% year-on-year revenue growth , and its acquisition of Hugging Face —was a dividend announcement. In May, Nvidia told shareholders that it would increase its quarterly payout to $0.25 per share from $0.01 per share. That’s a 2,400% increase, according to my calculations!\\nAnalysts will be quick to point out that perspective is key. “Nvidia’s dividend is virtually immaterial relative to its financial health and forward prospects,” wrote Brian Colello , who covers semiconductor stocks for Morningstar. Nvidia’s dividend yield will continue to register well below 1%. And even more of the company’s cash is being spent buying back shares.\\nBut in absolute terms, Nvidia’s dividend is huge. Something like $25 billion per year will be returned to shareholders. That sum will make Nvidia one of the largest dividend payers in the US stock market.\\nThe Nvidia news also comes at a time when the Morningstar US Dividend Growth Index is neck and neck with the broad US stock market, which is unusual in an up year. So, what’s going on with dividend growth stocks this year? And what does Nvidia’s dividend hike suggest, if anything?\\nDespite the dividend increase, it’s probably too soon to call Nvidia a dividend growth stock. Although the company initiated a quarterly dividend back in 2013, the payout was paltry until the 2026 increase. The Morningstar US Dividend Growth Index requires five consecutive years of dividend growth. Other dividend indexes on the market require an even longer history. Active managers running dividend growth strategies interpret their mandates in different ways.\\n“Most of Nvidia’s distributions to shareholders come in the form of share repurchases,” writes Colello. That’s consistent with the overall US stock market trend. As I wrote about recently , companies have spent far more buying back their own shares than they have on dividends.\\nIndeed, Nvidia announced an $80 billion share buyback program at the time of its dividend declaration. The company’s preference when it comes to cash allocations is clear in the exhibit below.\\nSource: Nvidia 10-K and 10-Q statements reconciled with Morningstar Indexes data by analyst Aryan Singh. Data as of July 26, 2026. Trailing 12-month data used. Buybacks depicted on a gross basis. Download CSV .\\nDividend growth has long been viewed as a symbol of corporate “quality.” Only profitable companies secure in their financial positions consistently increase cash returned to shareholders. While share buybacks can be opportunistic, dividends are a commitment. The market typically punishes dividend cutters.\\nThe nearly 400 stocks in the Morningstar US Dividend Growth Index include a mix of market leaders and less familiar names. Mega-caps like Microsoft MSFT , JPMorgan Chase JPM , Johnson & Johnson JNJ , and ExxonMobil XOM , which sit atop the index because constituents are weighted by market capitalization, are joined by mid- and small-cap stocks like Becton Dickinson BDX , DTE Energy DTE , and American Financial Group AFG .\\nInvestment style-wise, the dividend growth index leans toward value. Technology stocks, which tend to carry higher-than-average multiples, represent just 17.6% of index weight. That’s less than half their share of the broad US stock market. Meanwhile, the generally lower-priced financial services, healthcare, industrials, and consumer defensive sectors are overrepresented among dividend growers.\\nWhat about performance? Dividend growers have a reputation for being defensive stocks. Strong balance sheets and moderate price tags mean that dividend growers often shine during risk-off market environments.\\nIndeed, the Morningstar US Dividend Growth Index has generally outperformed during selloffs. It declined by less than the broad US stock market in down years 2018 and 2022. It also held its value relatively well during the tariff turmoil of March-April 2025 and in negative months like March and June 2026. From a volatility perspective, the dividend growth index has registered a standard deviation of returns well below the market.\\nGiven its defensive profile, it’s somewhat surprising that the dividend growth index is performing well in 2026. After all, the market is up double digits as the fourth quarter approaches. The technology sector has led this year, powered by the AI buildout. Those conditions aren’t typically favorable to dividend growers.\\nThe energy sector helps explain dividend growth’s strong returns. With the conflict in the Middle East pushing up oil prices, energy companies are booking huge profits. Key index constituents include ExxonMobil, with a near four-decade-long history of dividend growth, and ConocoPhillips COP , which rebuilt its dividend growth track record after low oil prices forced a dividend cut in 2016.\\nBut the biggest contributor to 2026 performance—both in absolute and relative terms—is the healthcare sector. Johnson & Johnson, Merck MRK , and UnitedHealth UNH are all having stellar years. J&J is a paragon of dividend growth going back to the 1960s. Merck and UnitedHealth have more modest histories in the neighborhood of 15 years, but UnitedHealth has posted double-digit dividend growth rates.\\nThe strong year for dividend growth reflects a number of trends. First, market leadership broadened from the narrow group of Magnificent Seven companies that dominated 2023-25. Nvidia, for example, has performed well, but not at the same level as in recent years. Amazon.com AMZN , Alphabet GOOGL , and Meta Platforms META have lagged. Second, this has been a year in which value stocks have outperformed growth. That includes the energy and healthcare sectors, but also value technology stocks benefiting from investments. Third, it has generally been a strong year for companies returning cash to shareholders, as I wrote about recently .\\nActually, this isn’t the first time dividend growers have outperformed in an up market. The dividend growth index also posted stellar returns in 2021, another year of market “breadth.” All US sectors posted strong returns then, and little separated growth and value stocks.\\nSome might see Nvidia’s dividend hike as a sign the company is entering a new, more slow-growth life stage. That doesn’t fit with the 70% revenue growth forecast. One thing that’s certain is that Nvidia’s cash pile is massive.\\nNvidia is also part of a trend I’ve written about regarding tech and tech-adjacent companies paying more dividends. Microsoft and Apple AAPL have both been constituents of the dividend growth index for years. In 2024, Meta and Alphabet both initiated payouts to shareholders.\\nNvidia also fits the dividend growth profile when it comes to profitability and financial strength. Increasing a payout can be seen as a sign of corporate quality. Because of their defensive characteristics, dividend growers are a good option for investors concerned about a market bubble .\\nMorningstar, Inc., licenses indexes to financial institutions as the tracking indexes for investable products, such as exchange-traded funds, sponsored by the financial institution. The license fee for such use is paid by the sponsoring financial institution based mainly on the total assets of the investable product. A list of ETFs that track a Morningstar index is available via the Capabilities section at indexes.morningstar.com. A list of other investable products linked to a Morningstar index is available upon request. Morningstar, Inc., does not market, sell, or make any representations regarding the advisability of investing in any investable product that tracks a Morningstar index.\",\n   \"named_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"figure\": 1,\n     \"text\": \"about $25 billion a year returned through the dividend\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"figure\": 2,\n     \"text\": \"the 2,400% increase of the dividend\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"figure\": 3,\n     \"text\": \"the $0.25 quarterly dividend\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"open_questions\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n     \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 3,\n   \"title\": \"Sandisk vs. NVIDIA: Which AI Stock Is the Better Buy Now?\",\n   \"publisher\": \"zacks.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"text\": \"Lately, surging artificial intelligence (AI) and data center demand has benefited both NVIDIA Corporation NVDA and the much smaller Sandisk Corporation SNDK. While the two companies offer different ways to capitalize on the AI boom, which one stands out to be the better buy now? Let's explore –\\nSandisk's AI-Driven Growth Boosts Revenues and Profitability\\nTotal revenues for Sandisk reached $8.97 billion in the fiscal fourth quarter of 2026, up a whopping 372% year over year and 51% sequentially, according to the company's Aug. 5 press release.\\nFor fiscal year 2026, revenues reached $20.25 billion, representing 175% year-over-year growth. The Data Center segment has been the major growth driver, with revenues jumping 437% as AI and data center demand increased the need for high-value storage.\\nIn the fourth fiscal quarter, revenue growth came more from higher pricing than higher volumes. As a result, gross margins rose to 84.6% compared to 26.2% a year ago.\\nFor fiscal year 2026, gross margin was 71.5% compared to 30.1% a year earlier. Profitability also improved dramatically, with GAAP net income coming in at $11.43 billion versus a loss of $1.64 billion in the previous year.\\nThe growth doesn't seem to be temporary, as management remains bullish on their near-term outlook. They expect revenues to be between $10.3 billion and $10.8 billion in the fiscal first quarter of 2027, which would mean another strong quarterly growth following a huge fiscal fourth quarter. Now that the company has signed five more New Business Model agreements, revenue growth and profitability will surely improve.\\nNVIDIA's AI Demand Drives Strong Growth and Margin Expansion\\nThe Data Center segment remains NVIDIA's primary growth engine, with revenues jumping 117% year over year and 18% sequentially to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the Aug. 26 press release. Consolidated revenues soared to $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially.\\nNVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin increased to 75% in the fiscal second quarter compared with 72.5% a year ago. Strong operating performance helped NVIDIA convert robust revenue growth into solid earnings growth.\\nNVIDIA further expects profitability to remain strong and projects revenues of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, in the fiscal third quarter of 2027. At the midpoint, this would represent a 12% sequential rise. Additionally, the next-generation Vera Rubin platform has entered the full production stage, positioning NVIDIA to benefit from the next phase of AI infrastructure investment.\\nBoth Sandisk and NVIDIA have delivered exceptional earnings growth, fueled by an increase in AI and data center demand, resulting in sharp revenue gains and substantial margin expansion. Both companies also look quite confident about their near-term outlooks.\\nHowever, Sandisk is in a highly cyclical memory business. If supply catches up with demand, memory prices may come under pressure and weigh on margins. Moreover, the company's fast-paced growth has raised expectations, which has made its growth trajectory susceptible if pricing momentum weakens.\\nSince Sandisk's business is dependent on storage demand, it has created greater concentration risk. In contrast, NVIDIA is capitalizing on the broader AI ecosystem, has dominant data center exposure, and continues to deliver exceptional AI-driven growth. To top it off, NVIDIA's 63.7% net profit margin compared with Sandisk's 56.5%, indicates its greater efficiency in converting revenues into bottom-line profits.\\nTaken together, these factors make NVIDIA a better buy than Sandisk at the moment. NVIDIA currently has a Zacks Rank #1 (Strong Buy), while Sandisk has a Zacks Rank #3 (Hold). You can see the complete list of today's Zacks Rank #1 stocks here.\",\n   \"named_figures\": [],\n   \"open_questions\": null\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 6,\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock Set For 40% Surge: 3 Catalysts That Could Drive the Next Big Move\",\n   \"publisher\": \"finance.yahoo.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-23\",\n   \"text\": \"NVDA earns a BUY with a $310.20 target (~40% upside), driven by the Vera Rubin ramp and $500 billion in mobilized AI infrastructure capital.\\nAMD's trailing P/E of 211 dwarfs NVDA's 45, while AVGO's AI semiconductor revenue surged 221%, confirming the AI infrastructure wave underpinning NVDA's outlook.\\nMy 24/7 Wall St. price target for NVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA ) is $310.20, which implies 39.56% upside from the current $225.35 quote. I rate NVDA a buy with high conviction.\\nThree catalysts drive the call: the Vera Rubin production ramp, more than $500 billion in third-party AI infrastructure capital being mobilized through NVIDIA partners, and the opening chapter of physical AI across robotics and autonomous vehicles.\\nNVDA is up 21.11% year to date and 28.17% over the trailing year. Q2 fiscal 2027 delivered revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29%. Data Center revenue hit $89.023 billion. Management guided Q3 to $108 billion.\\nWhy Bulls See $353 or Higher\\nThe bull-case one-year target is $353.54. Jensen Huang told investors NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand, with fiscal 2028 growth pegged at approximately 70%. Vera Rubin ramps platform value per gigawatt from Blackwell's $25 billion to $40 billion.\\nHyperscaler capex is expected to reach nearly 800 billion in 2026 and 1.3 trillion in 2027, a wave that also pulls in the power, cooling, and networking suppliers we profiled in a free report on seven AI infrastructure names that aren't chipmakers. Analyst sentiment shows 57 Buy or Strong Buy ratings against 2 Holds and 1 Sell.\\nThe bear scenario bottoms at $261.39, still above current price. Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\\nQ3 guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue citing geopolitical uncertainty. Gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in Q4 on memory cost inflation, recovering to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.\\nAdvanced Micro Devices ( NASDAQ:AMD ) posted Q2 fiscal 2026 revenue of $11.536 billion, up 50.11%, with Data Center up 107%. AMD trades at a trailing P/E of 211 versus NVDA's 45, making NVDA the cheaper name on growth per valuation dollar.\\nBroadcom ( NASDAQ:AVGO ) competes in custom AI ASICs and networking. Q3 fiscal 2026 revenue reached $29.591 billion, up 85.5%, with AI semiconductor revenue of $16.70 billion growing 221% year over year. Its acceleration validates NVDA's guided $108.0 billion quarter sits inside a real industry wave. The peer set makes $310.20 look reasonable.\\nI'd own NVDA at $225.35 with a 24/7 Wall St. price target of $310.20 and a buy rating at 90% confidence. Supply-constrained demand into a Vera Rubin ramp management calls the fastest in company history drives the call.\\nI'd stay a buyer if hyperscaler capex tracks toward 1.3 trillion in 2027. I'd step aside if memory pricing pushes gross margin below 71% or if China restrictions widen beyond the Data Center compute carve-out.\\nThese projections assume NVIDIA executes on the Vera Rubin ramp and hyperscaler AI spending holds. Significant upside or downside could result from broader China market access, a major AI capex reset, or accelerated adoption of physical AI and robotics platforms.\",\n   \"named_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"figure\": 1,\n     \"text\": \"fiscal 2028 growth of approximately 70%\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"figure\": 2,\n     \"text\": \"NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"open_questions\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n     \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n     \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n     \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\",\n     \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 7,\n   \"title\": \"What Does NVIDIA Offer That Texas Instruments Does Not?\",\n   \"publisher\": \"trefis.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-25\",\n   \"text\": \"What Does NVIDIA Offer That Texas Instruments Does Not?\\nTexas Instruments (TXN) and NVIDIA both sell chips for the data centers now being built. Data centers bring in nearly all of NVIDIA’s revenue, while TI sells to industrial and automotive customers as well as data centers. You pay 38.2 times the yearly profit for TI, yet only 28.1 times for NVIDIA. Their latest earnings calls show the two businesses parting ways on supply.\\nNVIDIA’s data center revenue was $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, out of $96 billion in total. Management guided fiscal Q3 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. That midpoint is about 12% above the Q2 total. Management expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028.\\nTI guided Q3 2026 revenue to $5.65 billion to $6.15 billion, against $5.5 billion in Q2. TI’s data center sales doubled from a year earlier in Q2. Management said most of the Q3 growth will come from selling more units, not from higher prices. TI also has clean room space it can equip if demand keeps rising.\\nThe two outlooks are different kinds of news. Demand for NVIDIA’s chips is larger than NVIDIA’s supply, so supply sets the pace. TI has spare room to make more, so demand sets TI’s pace.\\nEach company pays for its position. Memory prices rose more than NVIDIA expected, and NVIDIA reset its margin expectations. Gross margin is the share of sales left after production costs. NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\\nNVIDIA has also invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs. Management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA’s balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA’s business in fiscal 2028. That part of NVIDIA’s business would rest on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\\nTI’s cost is its factories. TI spent $3.3 billion on capital projects in the year to Q2 2026. TI’s $8.7 billion of cash from operations covered that more than twice over. TI’s free cash flow over the same period included $1.6 billion of CHIPS Act incentives. The yearly numbers on growth, margin, and debt show how far apart the two already are.\\nThis comparison scores six standard measures of price, growth, profit and debt , and NVIDIA leads Texas Instruments on all six. NVIDIA’s revenue grew 83% over the past year, against 16.7% for TI. NVIDIA kept 65.2 cents of each sales dollar as operating profit, against 38.1 cents at TI.\\nDebt equals 0.7% of NVIDIA’s market value, against 6.1% for TI. NVIDIA carries almost no debt against its value.\\nTI wins on one measure outside those six: price to sales. You pay 11.9 times the yearly revenue for TI, against 17.9 times for NVIDIA. The six scored measures judge price on profit, and on profit NVIDIA is cheaper.\\nTI also has a long dividend record. In September, TI raised the quarterly dividend to $1.52 a share, from $1.42. That was TI’s 23rd straight year of dividend increases.\\nWhat Would Count As A Stumble For NVIDIA?\\nNVIDIA’s lead is wide enough that it would take a real stumble to shrink it. Revenue below NVIDIA’s own guided range for fiscal Q3 would be the first sign of one. NVIDIA holds its call on that quarter on November 17. A result inside or above the range would leave NVIDIA’s lead in place.\\nNVIDIA leads on growth, margins, debt, and price on profit. TI’s strengths are a lower price on sales, spare capacity, and a long dividend record. Revenue below NVIDIA’s guided range in November is the first result that would test that lead.\\nHow To Act On TXN?\",\n   \"named_figures\": [],\n   \"open_questions\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n     \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n     \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n     \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\",\n     \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"article\": 8,\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia Would Have to Add Almost a Whole Broadcom to Reach $7 Trillion\",\n   \"publisher\": \"fool.com\",\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-09-25\",\n   \"text\": \"Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) , the world's most valuable company, is worth around $5.45 trillion as of this writing. The next big round number is $7 trillion. And the gap between them is larger than it sounds: roughly $1.55 trillion, or nearly the full market value of Broadcom ( AVGO +2.83% ) , the $1.69 trillion chip and infrastructure software business.\\nWhat would have to be true of Nvidia's revenue, its margins, and its price-to-earnings multiple for the company to add almost a whole Broadcom to its value? And how long could it take?\\nAfter all, fewer than 10 other companies in the world are worth more than the gap itself. At Nvidia's size, even a modest-sounding percentage gain today matches an entire giant company.\\nStep back, though, and the climb looks smaller than the dollars suggest. From around $225 as I write this, a $7 trillion market cap comes out to around $290 a share -- a rise of around 28%.\\nFor context, shares have climbed 37% from their 52-week low, and they now trade within around 5% of their 52-week high. I'd say nothing about a 28% move is unusual for this stock.\\nNvidia's latest results make the growth requirement seem nearly routine. Revenue in the fiscal 2027 second quarter (the period ended July 26, 2026) came to $96.2 billion, up 106% over a year earlier. This pace was even faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth. The data center business made up $89 billion of the total. And net income , at $59.7 billion, rose 126% year over year.\\n\\\"Now, compute is revenue,\\\" said CEO Jensen Huang in the company's second-quarter earnings release.\\nThe forecasts go far past the quarter. Guidance for the fiscal third quarter is $108 billion in revenue , around 12% above fiscal Q2. And on the company's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said Nvidia expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028. She called it \\\"a supply-constrained outlook.\\\" Put another way, the limit is manufacturing capacity, not customer demand.\\nAnalysts' consensus already has fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion. Grow that by roughly 70%, and fiscal 2028 lands somewhere around $700 billion.\\nMargins might not change the picture much, either. On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\\nWhen might it happen?\\nNvidia's stock costs around 14 times forward earnings today, using analysts' fiscal 2028 estimates. At $290 per share, that forward price-to-earnings multiple would sit closer to 18 -- around what Broadcom gets today on the same basis.\\nFiscal 2028 ends in January 2028, roughly 16 months from now. If Nvidia delivers the year it has forecast, and the market prices those earnings the way it prices Broadcom's today, $7 trillion arrives somewhere on that timeline.\\nThe stock could also get there without any increase in its price-to-earnings multiple. Hold that multiple of expected earnings flat, and shares hit $290 when expectations for fiscal 2029 are around 28% over fiscal 2028's. Next to this fiscal year's expected 90% revenue growth and next year's forecast 70%, that would arguably be a low bar. But nothing about fiscal 2029 is guided yet. And estimates that far out can change a lot.\\nOf course, the valuation multiple is also the half Nvidia doesn't control. Shares have risen roughly 21% in 2026 as of this writing, a year when quarterly profit more than doubled. That mismatch is the price-to-earnings multiple shrinking, quarter by quarter.\\nIf the shrinking continues at anything like this year's pace, no forecast growth rate could take the stock to $7 trillion on this timeline.\\nIn the end, what separates this milestone from past ones is that the growth to support it is already forecast. Reaching $7 trillion doesn't need a reacceleration or a richer valuation than Broadcom gets today.\\nI think the earnings will show up more or less as forecast. What the market chooses to pay for them is another question.\\nBy Daniel Sparks – Sep 25, 2026 at 7:24PM EST\\nNvidia's market value is roughly $5.45 trillion as of this writing, leaving a gap of roughly $1.55 trillion to $7 trillion.\",\n   \"named_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"figure\": 1,\n     \"text\": \"the fiscal second quarter's 75% gross margin\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"figure\": 2,\n     \"text\": \"gross margin of 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"open_questions\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-1\",\n     \"question\": \"What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-2\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?\",\n     \"period\": \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-3\",\n     \"question\": \"Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-4\",\n     \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\",\n     \"period\": \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-5\",\n     \"question\": \"How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?\",\n     \"period\": \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"clean-6\",\n     \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA complete its approximately $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, agreed in 2026, by the check date?\",\n     \"period\": \"Check date 24 March 2028 (18 months after the 25 September 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a sized acquisition not yet completed, because the item gives no signing or closing date; also observable in NVIDIA's quarterly filings before then.\"\n    }\n   ]\n  }\n ],\n \"answers\": [\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B07\",\n   \"article\": 1,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported that fiscal second-quarter revenue more than doubled year over year to $96.2 billion, with data center revenue up 117% to $89 billion, as reported by Barchart.com.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Fiscal second-quarter revenue more than doubled year over year to $96.2 billion, with data center revenue surging 117% to $89 billion\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"more than doubled year over year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"data center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"up 117% year over year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided for approximately $108 billion in third-quarter revenue, which Barchart.com said implies 89% growth.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"management has guided for approximately $108 billion in third-quarter revenue, implying 89% growth\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"third quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"approximately $108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"implied revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"third quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"89%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"implied\",\n       \"party\": \"Barchart.com\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress guided for 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028, far exceeding the 45% growth Wall Street had previously modeled, according to Barchart.com.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"CFO Colette Kress guided for 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — a figure that far exceeded the 45% Wall Street had previously modeled\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA (Colette Kress)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth previously modeled by Wall Street\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"45%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"previously modeled\",\n       \"party\": \"Wall Street\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e5\",\n     \"summary\": \"Barchart.com estimated that roughly 25% of NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 revenue is expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing, potentially $175 billion funded off NVIDIA's own balance sheet.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off its own balance sheet.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Barchart.com\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"share of revenue from customers receiving company-backed financing\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly 25%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"share of NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 revenue\",\n       \"party\": \"Barchart.com\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"company-backed financing funded off its own balance sheet\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"$175 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"potentially\",\n       \"party\": \"Barchart.com\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Provides an estimate of the scale of NVIDIA's company-backed financing to AI-startup customers (roughly 25% of FY2028 revenue, potentially $175 billion), informing the total credit support and revenue backstops to be disclosed at the next annual report.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e6\",\n     \"summary\": \"Barchart.com reported that NVIDIA's off-balance-sheet guarantees exceed $100 billion, tied to projects such as the SB Energy campus for OpenAI.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Additionally, off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects like the SB Energy campus for OpenAI introduce credit risk that traditional valuation metrics do not capture.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Barchart.com\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"off-balance-sheet guarantees\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"exceeding $100 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"tied to projects like the SB Energy campus for OpenAI\",\n       \"party\": \"Barchart.com\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Gives an estimate of NVIDIA's off-balance-sheet guarantee exposure (over $100 billion) tied to AI-lab projects like SB Energy for OpenAI, informing the credit-support and guarantee total to be disclosed at the next annual report.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e7\",\n     \"summary\": \"Barchart.com reported that analysts' earnings estimates for NVIDIA were revised sharply higher over the past nine months, from $8.50 to $15.68 per share for fiscal 2029.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"earnings estimates that have been revised sharply higher — from $8.50 to $15.68 per share for fiscal 2029 — over just the past nine months.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"consensus of analysts\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"EPS estimate\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2029\",\n       \"value\": \"$15.68 per share\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"revised from $8.50 per share over the past nine months\",\n       \"party\": \"consensus\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B10\",\n   \"article\": 1,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's fiscal second-quarter revenue more than doubled year over year to $96.2 billion, with data center revenue up 117% to $89 billion, as reported in the article.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Fiscal second-quarter revenue more than doubled year over year to $96.2 billion, with data center revenue surging 117% to $89 billion,\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"more than doubled year over year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"data center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"surging 117%\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided third-quarter revenue to approximately $108 billion, implying 89% growth, per the article.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"management has guided for approximately $108 billion in third-quarter revenue, implying 89% growth.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"third quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"approximately; implies 89% growth\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress guided for 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028, far above the 45% growth Wall Street had previously modeled, according to the article.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"while CFO Colette Kress guided for 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — a figure that far exceeded the 45% Wall Street had previously modeled.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"company guidance\",\n       \"party\": \"Colette Kress (NVIDIA CFO)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth previously modeled\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"45%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"prior consensus model\",\n       \"party\": \"Wall Street\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n     \"summary\": \"The article states NVIDIA is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing, potentially $175 billion funded off NVIDIA's own balance sheet.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off its own balance sheet.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"barchart.com\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"share of revenue expected from customers receiving company-backed financing\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"25%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"roughly; article's estimate\",\n       \"party\": \"barchart.com\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue potentially funded off NVIDIA's own balance sheet\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"$175 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"potentially; article's estimate\",\n       \"party\": \"barchart.com\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Quantifies, as the article's estimate, the share of NVIDIA revenue tied to company-backed customer financing and the amount funded off its balance sheet — directly bearing on the open question of how much credit support and revenue backstop NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners and will disclose.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-6\",\n     \"summary\": \"The article states NVIDIA carries off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects such as the SB Energy campus for OpenAI, which it says introduce credit risk not captured by traditional valuation metrics.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Additionally, off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects like the SB Energy campus for OpenAI introduce credit risk that traditional valuation metrics do not capture.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"barchart.com\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"off-balance-sheet guarantees\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$100 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"exceeding; tied to projects like the SB Energy campus for OpenAI\",\n       \"party\": \"barchart.com\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Gives a figure (exceeding $100 billion) for NVIDIA's off-balance-sheet guarantees tied to AI-project campuses, the exact quantity the open thesis tracks for disclosure at the next annual report; it is the article's characterization, not the company's reported disclosure.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-7\",\n     \"summary\": \"The article reports that consensus earnings estimates for NVIDIA have been revised sharply higher over just the past nine months, from $8.50 to $15.68 per share for fiscal 2029.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"earnings estimates that have been revised sharply higher — from $8.50 to $15.68 per share for fiscal 2029 — over just the past nine months.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"consensus\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"consensus EPS estimate (prior)\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2029\",\n       \"value\": \"$8.50\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"per share; level before revision over the past nine months\",\n       \"party\": \"consensus\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"consensus EPS estimate (current)\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2029\",\n       \"value\": \"$15.68 per share\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"per share; revised sharply higher over the past nine months\",\n       \"party\": \"consensus\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B19\",\n   \"article\": 6,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported fiscal 2027 second-quarter revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29%, and Data Center revenue of $89.023 billion, as recounted in 24/7 Wall St.'s 23 September 2026 article.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Q2 fiscal 2027 delivered revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29%. Data Center revenue hit $89.023 billion.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.22 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"105.85%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"year over year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"non-GAAP EPS\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$2.22\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP; beat consensus by 6.29%\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"Data Center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$89.023 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Data Center segment\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided fiscal 2027 third-quarter revenue to $108 billion, and the article states that this Q3 guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue, citing geopolitical uncertainty.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Management guided Q3 to $108 billion.\",\n      \"Q3 guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue citing geopolitical uncertainty.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Q3 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"excludes China Data Center compute revenue citing geopolitical uncertainty\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-3\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"NVIDIA's Q3 fiscal 2027 guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue on geopolitical uncertainty, i.e. China data-center compute revenue was not assumed to resume in the guided quarter; this bears on whether NVIDIA's current-generation chips can be sold into China by the end of fiscal 2027.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n     \"summary\": \"The article states NVIDIA's gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in Q4 on memory cost inflation, recovering to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in Q4 on memory cost inflation, recovering to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"Q4\",\n       \"value\": \"71% to 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"range; expected to bottom on memory cost inflation\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"72% to 73%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"range; as volumes scale\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n     \"summary\": \"24/7 Wall St. lists what it calls NVIDIA's real risks as supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and days sales outstanding extending to 60 days.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"24/7 Wall St.\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-09-23\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"supply commitments\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$279 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"24/7 Wall St.\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"guarantee obligations (capped)\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$108.5 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"capped\",\n       \"party\": \"24/7 Wall St.\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"days sales outstanding (DSO)\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"60 days\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"described as extending\",\n       \"party\": \"24/7 Wall St.\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-2\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"States supply commitments of $279 billion, a reported baseline for the purchase/supply commitment amount the thesis tracks at NVIDIA's next annual report; the figure is the article's, not a citation to a filing.\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"States guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, addressing the amount of guarantees/credit support the thesis tracks at NVIDIA's next annual report; the figure is the article's characterisation and not a filing citation.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang told investors that NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand, with fiscal 2028 growth pegged at approximately 70%, according to the article.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Jensen Huang told investors NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand, with fiscal 2028 growth pegged at approximately 70%.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Jensen Huang (NVIDIA)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"share of demand NVIDIA can supply\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Jensen Huang\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"growth\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"approximately 70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"described as 'pegged at'\",\n       \"party\": \"Jensen Huang\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B21\",\n   \"article\": 7,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported data center revenue of $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027 out of $96 billion in total revenue, and management guided fiscal Q3 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, a midpoint the Trefis article calculates as about 12% above the Q2 total.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA’s data center revenue was $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, out of $96 billion in total. Management guided fiscal Q3 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. That midpoint is about 12% above the Q2 total.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"data center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"data center segment\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"total revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q2 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$96 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q3 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"plus or minus 2%\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA management\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA said memory prices rose more than it expected and it reset its margin expectations, guiding gross margin to 74% for fiscal Q3 2027, down from 75% in Q2, and expecting gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Memory prices rose more than NVIDIA expected, and NVIDIA reset its margin expectations.\",\n      \"NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q3\",\n       \"value\": \"74%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal Q4\",\n       \"value\": \"71% to 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"expected bottom\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs, and management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA's balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA's business in fiscal 2028 — a portion of its business resting on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA has also invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs. Management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA’s balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA’s business in fiscal 2028. That part of NVIDIA’s business would rest on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"investment in AI labs\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"nearly $50 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"share of NVIDIA's business from AI labs backed by NVIDIA's balance sheet\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly a quarter\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"AI labs backed by NVIDIA's balance sheet\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA management\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Gives the scale of NVIDIA's equity investment in AI labs (nearly $50 billion) alongside management's expectation that balance-sheet-backed labs reach roughly a quarter of its fiscal 2028 business, informing the thesis on how much credit support and revenue backstop NVIDIA discloses versus equity investments.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n     \"summary\": \"Trefis calculated that NVIDIA's revenue grew 83% over the past year, against 16.7% for Texas Instruments, and that NVIDIA kept 65.2 cents of each sales dollar as operating profit, against 38.1 cents at TI.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA’s revenue grew 83% over the past year, against 16.7% for TI. NVIDIA kept 65.2 cents of each sales dollar as operating profit, against 38.1 cents at TI.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"trefis.com\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-09-25\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"over the past year\",\n       \"value\": \"83%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"trefis.com\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"operating profit as a share of sales\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"65.2 cents\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"of each sales dollar\",\n       \"party\": \"trefis.com\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B29\",\n   \"article\": 8,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported fiscal 2027 second-quarter revenue (quarter ended July 26, 2026) of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year — faster than the 85% year-over-year growth of its fiscal first quarter — with the data center business contributing $89 billion of the total and net income of $59.7 billion, up 126% year over year, as stated in the company's second-quarter earnings release.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Revenue in the fiscal 2027 second quarter (the period ended July 26, 2026) came to $96.2 billion, up 106% over a year earlier. This pace was even faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth. The data center business made up $89 billion of the total. And net income , at $59.7 billion, rose 126% year over year.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 second quarter (period ended July 26, 2026)\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"company-reported, fiscal 2027 second quarter ended July 26, 2026\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"106%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 first quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"85%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"Data center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"data center segment, part of total revenue\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 5,\n       \"measure\": \"Net income\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$59.7 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 6,\n       \"measure\": \"Net income growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"126%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA's guidance for its fiscal third quarter is $108 billion in revenue, around 12% above fiscal second-quarter revenue, as cited by fool.com.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Guidance for the fiscal third quarter is $108 billion in revenue , around 12% above fiscal Q2.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal third quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Guidance growth versus fiscal Q2 revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal third quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"around 12%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"sequential, versus fiscal Q2\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e3\",\n     \"summary\": \"On NVIDIA's late-August 2026 earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said the company expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending January 2028, and described it as \\\"a supply-constrained outlook.\\\"\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"And on the company's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said Nvidia expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028. She called it \\\"a supply-constrained outlook.\\\"\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"late August 2026\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028 (year ending January 2028)\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly 70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"described by the CFO as \\\"a supply-constrained outlook\\\"\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-2\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"The CFO's statement that fiscal 2028 growth is limited by manufacturing capacity, not customer demand, bears on how much NVIDIA must commit to purchase and supply capacity for that growth; it does not itself quantify any purchase or supply commitment.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e4\",\n     \"summary\": \"On the late-August 2026 earnings call, NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes take effect, a decline from the fiscal second quarter's 75%.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"late August 2026\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin (bottom)\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal fourth quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"between 71% and 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin (settling level)\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"72% to 73%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"as price hikes kick in\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e5\",\n     \"summary\": \"Analysts' consensus has NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion, as reported by fool.com.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Analysts' consensus already has fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"consensus (analysts)\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$412 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"analysts' consensus\",\n       \"party\": \"consensus\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B37\",\n   \"article\": 3,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA, in its Aug. 26 press release, reported fiscal second-quarter 2027 consolidated revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially, with Data Center segment revenue of $89 billion, up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially; non-GAAP gross margin rose to 75% from 72.5% a year earlier.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"The Data Center segment remains NVIDIA's primary growth engine, with revenues jumping 117% year over year and 18% sequentially to $89 billion in the fiscal second quarter of 2027, according to the Aug. 26 press release. Consolidated revenues soared to $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially.\",\n      \"NVIDIA's non-GAAP gross margin increased to 75% in the fiscal second quarter compared with 72.5% a year ago.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Consolidated revenues\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"consolidated; up 106% year over year and 18% sequentially\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Data Center segment revenues\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Data Center segment; up 117% year over year and 18% sequentially\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Data Center revenue growth, year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"117%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Data Center segment, year over year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"Non-GAAP gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP; versus 72.5% a year ago\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided fiscal third-quarter 2027 revenues to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, which at the midpoint the article says would be a 12% sequential rise; the company said it further expects profitability to remain strong.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"NVIDIA further expects profitability to remain strong and projects revenues of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, in the fiscal third quarter of 2027. At the midpoint, this would represent a 12% sequential rise.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-08-26\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenues (guidance)\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal third quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"plus or minus 2%\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Sequential revenue growth at guidance midpoint\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal third quarter of 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"12%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"sequential rise at the midpoint of guidance; article's calculation\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B38\",\n   \"article\": 6,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e1\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported fiscal Q2 2027 revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29% and Data Center revenue of $89.023 billion, per 24/7 Wall St.'s buy note.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Q2 fiscal 2027 delivered revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29%. Data Center revenue hit $89.023 billion.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.22 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"105.85%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"non-GAAP EPS\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$2.22\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP; beat consensus by 6.29%\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"Data Center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$89.023 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Data Center segment\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e2\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA management guided fiscal Q3 2027 revenue to $108 billion, according to 24/7 Wall St.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Management guided Q3 to $108 billion.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Q3 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e4\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang told investors the company can supply only 70% of demand, with fiscal 2028 growth pegged at approximately 70%, as reported by 24/7 Wall St.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Jensen Huang told investors NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand, with fiscal 2028 growth pegged at approximately 70%.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"growth\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"approximately 70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"pegged at approximately 70%\",\n       \"party\": \"Jensen Huang\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e5\",\n     \"summary\": \"24/7 Wall St. cites NVIDIA's Vera Rubin production ramp as one of three catalysts for its buy call, saying Vera Rubin lifts platform value per gigawatt to $40 billion from Blackwell's $25 billion and citing management's description of the ramp as the fastest in company history; the item gives no shipment date or volume.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Vera Rubin ramps platform value per gigawatt from Blackwell's $25 billion to $40 billion.\",\n      \"Supply-constrained demand into a Vera Rubin ramp management calls the fastest in company history drives the call.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"platform value per gigawatt (Vera Rubin)\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$40 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"platform value per gigawatt, versus Blackwell\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"platform value per gigawatt (Blackwell)\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$25 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Blackwell comparison basis cited for Vera Rubin\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-4\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Reports that the Vera Rubin ramp is under way, with management calling it the fastest in company history, which bears on whether Vera Rubin reaches volume customer shipments in fiscal 2027; the item gives no shipment or revenue-recognition date, so it does not establish the milestone.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e7\",\n     \"summary\": \"24/7 Wall St. lists supply commitments of $279 billion among the real risks in the NVIDIA case; the item does not say on what date or in which filing that amount was reported.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"24/7 Wall St.\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"supply commitments\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$279 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"presented as an existing risk; source and as-of date of the figure not stated in the item\",\n       \"party\": \"24/7 Wall St.\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-2\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Gives a figure for NVIDIA's supply commitments ($279 billion) that the thesis tracks against the purchase and supply commitment amount disclosed in the next Form 10-K; the item gives no as-of date, so it cannot be tied to a specific reporting period.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e8\",\n     \"summary\": \"24/7 Wall St. lists guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion among the real risks in the NVIDIA case; the item gives no as-of date or source for the figure.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"24/7 Wall St.\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"guarantee obligations (capped)\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$108.5 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"capped; source and as-of date not stated in the item\",\n       \"party\": \"24/7 Wall St.\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"States a $108.5 billion cap on NVIDIA's guarantee obligations, the exact quantity the thesis tracks at the next annual report; the item does not say whether this reflects disclosed guarantees, credit support for AI labs and cloud partners, or the analyst's own aggregation.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e9\",\n     \"summary\": \"24/7 Wall St. says NVIDIA's days sales outstanding are extending to 60 days and lists this among the real risks in the case.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"24/7 Wall St.\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"days sales outstanding (DSO)\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"60 days\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"described as extending\",\n       \"party\": \"24/7 Wall St.\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"e13\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA expects gross margins to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in fiscal Q4 2027 on memory cost inflation, then recover to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale, as reported by 24/7 Wall St.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in Q4 on memory cost inflation, recovering to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"Q4 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"71% to 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"expected to bottom, on memory cost inflation\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"72% to 73%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"recovery as volumes scale\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B39\",\n   \"article\": 6,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-q2-fy27-results\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA reported Q2 fiscal 2027 revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29%; Data Center revenue was $89.023 billion.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Q2 fiscal 2027 delivered revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29%. Data Center revenue hit $89.023 billion.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.22 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"105.85%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"year over year\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"non-GAAP EPS\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$2.22\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"non-GAAP\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"Data Center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"Q2 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$89.023 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"Data Center\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-q3-fy27-guidance-china-exclusion\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA guided Q3 fiscal 2027 revenue to $108 billion; the Q3 guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue, citing geopolitical uncertainty.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Management guided Q3 to $108 billion.\",\n      \"Q3 guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue citing geopolitical uncertainty.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"Q3 fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"excludes China Data Center compute revenue\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-3\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"The guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue, showing China data-center sales are not in the Q3 outlook and bearing on whether NVIDIA can resume China sales in FY2027.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-gross-margin-guidance\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71%–72% in Q4 fiscal 2027 on memory cost inflation, then recover to 72%–73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in Q4 on memory cost inflation, recovering to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"Q4\",\n       \"value\": \"71% to 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"on memory cost inflation\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"72% to 73%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"as volumes scale\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-supply-guarantees-dso\",\n     \"summary\": \"24/7 Wall St. lists NVIDIA's risk exposures as supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and days sales outstanding extending to 60 days.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"supply commitments\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$279 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"guarantee obligations\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$108.5 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"capped at\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"days sales outstanding (DSO)\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"60 days\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"extending to\",\n       \"party\": \"NVIDIA\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-2\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Reports $279 billion of supply commitments, directly the purchase/supply commitment amount clean-2 tracks at the next annual report.\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Reports guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, directly the credit-support/guarantee amount clean-1 tracks at the next annual report.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-jensen-supply-demand-growth\",\n     \"summary\": \"NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang told investors NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand and pegged fiscal 2028 growth at approximately 70%.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Jensen Huang told investors NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand, with fiscal 2028 growth pegged at approximately 70%.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Jensen Huang\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"share of demand NVIDIA can supply\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Jensen Huang\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"fiscal 2028 growth\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"approximately 70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Jensen Huang\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-4\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"The CEO's statement that NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand and his ~70% fiscal 2028 growth forecast indicate demand exceeds supply, a demand signal for the Vera Rubin ramp; it does not confirm shipment timing.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-vera-rubin-platform-value\",\n     \"summary\": \"24/7 Wall St. says Vera Rubin raises NVIDIA's platform value per gigawatt to $40 billion from Blackwell's $25 billion.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Vera Rubin ramps platform value per gigawatt from Blackwell's $25 billion to $40 billion.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"24/7 Wall St.\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"2026-09-23\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"platform value per gigawatt (Blackwell)\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$25 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Blackwell\",\n       \"party\": \"24/7 Wall St.\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"platform value per gigawatt (Vera Rubin)\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$40 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"Vera Rubin\",\n       \"party\": \"24/7 Wall St.\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-4\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Provides an estimate of Vera Rubin's platform value per gigawatt versus Blackwell, informing the economics of the Vera Rubin ramp tracked by the thesis; it does not establish shipment timing.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B53\",\n   \"article\": 2,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia told shareholders in May that it would raise its quarterly dividend to $0.25 per share from $0.01 per share, an increase the Morningstar author calculates at 2,400%.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"In May, Nvidia told shareholders that it would increase its quarterly payout to $0.25 per share from $0.01 per share. That’s a 2,400% increase, according to my calculations!\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"May\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"quarterly dividend per share\",\n       \"period\": \"quarterly\",\n       \"value\": \"$0.25 per share\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"prior quarterly dividend per share\",\n       \"period\": \"quarterly\",\n       \"value\": \"$0.01 per share\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"increase in quarterly dividend\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"2,400%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"author's calculation\",\n       \"party\": \"Morningstar\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia announced an $80 billion share buyback program at the time of its dividend declaration, according to the Morningstar article.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Indeed, Nvidia announced an $80 billion share buyback program at the time of its dividend declaration.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"share buyback program\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$80 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n     \"summary\": \"The Morningstar article refers to Nvidia's own forecasts of 70% year-on-year revenue growth, a rate the author says is inconsistent with the idea that the company is entering a slow-growth stage; the item does not state which period the 70% growth forecast covers.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Lost in a torrent of news about Nvidia NVDA this year—its role in financing $500 billion in artificial intelligence investments , its forecasts of 70% year-on-year revenue growth , and its acquisition of Hugging Face —was a dividend announcement.\",\n      \"That doesn’t fit with the 70% revenue growth forecast.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"year-on-year revenue growth forecast\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n     \"summary\": \"The Morningstar article describes Nvidia's role in financing $500 billion in artificial intelligence investments as one of the major Nvidia news items of the year; it does not break out how much of that is guarantees, credit support or direct equity investment.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Lost in a torrent of news about Nvidia NVDA this year—its role in financing $500 billion in artificial intelligence investments , its forecasts of 70% year-on-year revenue growth , and its acquisition of Hugging Face —was a dividend announcement.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"artificial intelligence investments Nvidia has a role in financing\",\n       \"period\": null,\n       \"value\": \"$500 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"as described by the article; no split between guarantees, credit support and equity investment\",\n       \"party\": null\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-1\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"Indicates the scale of Nvidia's financial involvement in AI investments ($500 billion) that the annual-report disclosure of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops would quantify; the article does not separate guarantees or credit support from equity investment, so it speaks to magnitude only.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  {\n   \"answer\": \"B59\",\n   \"article\": 8,\n   \"statements_with_figures\": [\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-1\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia reported fiscal 2027 second-quarter (period ended July 26, 2026) revenue of $96.2 billion, up 106% year over year — faster than the 85% year-over-year growth of its fiscal first quarter — with the data center business accounting for $89 billion of the total and net income of $59.7 billion, up 126% year over year, per the company's second-quarter earnings release as reported by fool.com.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Nvidia's latest results make the growth requirement seem nearly routine. Revenue in the fiscal 2027 second quarter (the period ended July 26, 2026) came to $96.2 billion, up 106% over a year earlier. This pace was even faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth. The data center business made up $89 billion of the total. And net income , at $59.7 billion, rose 126% year over year.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 second quarter (period ended July 26, 2026)\",\n       \"value\": \"$96.2 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"company total\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth year over year\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"106%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Data center revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$89 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": \"data center business, of company total\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 4,\n       \"measure\": \"Net income\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027 second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$59.7 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-2\",\n     \"summary\": \"Nvidia guided fiscal 2027 third-quarter revenue to $108 billion, around 12% above its fiscal second-quarter revenue, as reported by fool.com.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Guidance for the fiscal third quarter is $108 billion in revenue , around 12% above fiscal Q2.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue guidance\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal third quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"$108 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"around 12% above fiscal Q2\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-3\",\n     \"summary\": \"On Nvidia's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said the company expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028 (the year ending January 2028), describing it as \\\"a supply-constrained outlook\\\" in which the limit is manufacturing capacity rather than customer demand, as reported by fool.com.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"And on the company's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said Nvidia expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028. She called it \\\"a supply-constrained outlook.\\\" Put another way, the limit is manufacturing capacity, not customer demand.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"late August\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"roughly 70%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"\\\"a supply-constrained outlook\\\"\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia (CFO Colette Kress)\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": [\n      {\n       \"question\": \"clean-2\",\n       \"new_question\": false,\n       \"reason\": \"The CFO's statement that the fiscal 2028 outlook is constrained by manufacturing capacity rather than demand bears on whether Nvidia must lock in large purchase and supply commitments; the item gives no amount of such commitments, so it does not establish their size.\"\n      }\n     ]\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-4\",\n     \"summary\": \"On Nvidia's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes take effect, down from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, as reported by fool.com.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Nvidia\",\n     \"statement_date\": \"late August\",\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal fourth quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"between 71% and 72%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"bottom level\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia (CFO Colette Kress)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2028\",\n       \"value\": \"72% to 73%\",\n       \"kind\": \"guidance\",\n       \"basis\": \"steady level as price hikes kick in\",\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia (CFO Colette Kress)\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 3,\n       \"measure\": \"Gross margin\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal second quarter\",\n       \"value\": \"75%\",\n       \"kind\": \"actual\",\n       \"basis\": null,\n       \"party\": \"Nvidia\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"id\": \"ev-5\",\n     \"summary\": \"According to analyst consensus cited by fool.com, Nvidia's fiscal 2027 revenue is expected to come in around $412 billion, implying roughly 90% revenue growth for the fiscal year; the article uses those consensus figures as the base for its valuation arithmetic.\",\n     \"passages\": [\n      \"Analysts' consensus already has fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion.\",\n      \"Next to this fiscal year's expected 90% revenue growth and next year's forecast 70%, that would arguably be a low bar.\"\n     ],\n     \"statement_party\": \"Analyst consensus\",\n     \"statement_date\": null,\n     \"figures\": [\n      {\n       \"n\": 1,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue\",\n       \"period\": \"fiscal 2027\",\n       \"value\": \"around $412 billion\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"analysts' consensus\",\n       \"party\": \"consensus\"\n      },\n      {\n       \"n\": 2,\n       \"measure\": \"Revenue growth\",\n       \"period\": \"this fiscal year (fiscal 2027)\",\n       \"value\": \"90%\",\n       \"kind\": \"estimate\",\n       \"basis\": \"expected growth\",\n       \"party\": \"consensus\"\n      }\n     ],\n     \"links\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ]\n  }\n ]\n}","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields/reader-packet-1-settle.json\\\")); [(print(\\\"ARTICLE\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"],a[\\\"text\\\"],\\\"QUESTIONS\\\",a.get(\\\"open_questions\\\"),\\\"NAMED\\\",a.get(\\\"named_figures\\\"))) for a in p[\\\"articles\\\"] if a[\\\"article\\\"] not in (1,2,3)]; print(\\\"ANSWERS\\\"); [(print(\\\"ANSWER\\\",a[\\\"answer\\\"],\\\"ARTICLE\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"]),[(print(\\\"STATEMENT\\\",s[\\\"id\\\"],\\\"PARTY\\\",s.get(\\\"statement_party\\\"),\\\"DATE\\\",s.get(\\\"statement_date\\\"),\\\"PASSAGES\\\",s.get(\\\"passages\\\"),\\\"FIGURES\\\",s[\\\"figures\\\"],\\\"LINKS\\\",s.get(\\\"links\\\",\\\"ABSENT\\\"))) for s in a[\\\"statements_with_figures\\\"]]) for a in p[\\\"answers\\\"]]' \"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/figure-fields/reader-packet-1-settle.json\\\")); [(print(\\\"ARTICLE\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"],a[\\\"text\\\"],\\\"QUESTIONS\\\",a.get(\\\"open_questions\\\"),\\\"NAMED\\\",a.get(\\\"named_figures\\\"))) for a in p[\\\"articles\\\"] if a[\\\"article\\\"] not in (1,2,3)]; print(\\\"ANSWERS\\\"); [(print(\\\"ANSWER\\\",a[\\\"answer\\\"],\\\"ARTICLE\\\",a[\\\"article\\\"]),[(print(\\\"STATEMENT\\\",s[\\\"id\\\"],\\\"PARTY\\\",s.get(\\\"statement_party\\\"),\\\"DATE\\\",s.get(\\\"statement_date\\\"),\\\"PASSAGES\\\",s.get(\\\"passages\\\"),\\\"FIGURES\\\",s[\\\"figures\\\"],\\\"LINKS\\\",s.get(\\\"links\\\",\\\"ABSENT\\\"))) for s in a[\\\"statements_with_figures\\\"]]) for a in p[\\\"answers\\\"]]' \"","aggregated_output":"ARTICLE 6 NVDA earns a BUY with a $310.20 target (~40% upside), driven by the Vera Rubin ramp and $500 billion in mobilized AI infrastructure capital.\nAMD's trailing P/E of 211 dwarfs NVDA's 45, while AVGO's AI semiconductor revenue surged 221%, confirming the AI infrastructure wave underpinning NVDA's outlook.\nMy 24/7 Wall St. price target for NVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA ) is $310.20, which implies 39.56% upside from the current $225.35 quote. I rate NVDA a buy with high conviction.\nThree catalysts drive the call: the Vera Rubin production ramp, more than $500 billion in third-party AI infrastructure capital being mobilized through NVIDIA partners, and the opening chapter of physical AI across robotics and autonomous vehicles.\nNVDA is up 21.11% year to date and 28.17% over the trailing year. Q2 fiscal 2027 delivered revenue of $96.22 billion, up 105.85% year over year, with non-GAAP EPS of $2.22 beating consensus by 6.29%. Data Center revenue hit $89.023 billion. Management guided Q3 to $108 billion.\nWhy Bulls See $353 or Higher\nThe bull-case one-year target is $353.54. Jensen Huang told investors NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand, with fiscal 2028 growth pegged at approximately 70%. Vera Rubin ramps platform value per gigawatt from Blackwell's $25 billion to $40 billion.\nHyperscaler capex is expected to reach nearly 800 billion in 2026 and 1.3 trillion in 2027, a wave that also pulls in the power, cooling, and networking suppliers we profiled in a free report on seven AI infrastructure names that aren't chipmakers. Analyst sentiment shows 57 Buy or Strong Buy ratings against 2 Holds and 1 Sell.\nThe bear scenario bottoms at $261.39, still above current price. Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\nQ3 guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue citing geopolitical uncertainty. Gross margins are expected to bottom in the 71% to 72% range in Q4 on memory cost inflation, recovering to 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as volumes scale.\nAdvanced Micro Devices ( NASDAQ:AMD ) posted Q2 fiscal 2026 revenue of $11.536 billion, up 50.11%, with Data Center up 107%. AMD trades at a trailing P/E of 211 versus NVDA's 45, making NVDA the cheaper name on growth per valuation dollar.\nBroadcom ( NASDAQ:AVGO ) competes in custom AI ASICs and networking. Q3 fiscal 2026 revenue reached $29.591 billion, up 85.5%, with AI semiconductor revenue of $16.70 billion growing 221% year over year. Its acceleration validates NVDA's guided $108.0 billion quarter sits inside a real industry wave. The peer set makes $310.20 look reasonable.\nI'd own NVDA at $225.35 with a 24/7 Wall St. price target of $310.20 and a buy rating at 90% confidence. Supply-constrained demand into a Vera Rubin ramp management calls the fastest in company history drives the call.\nI'd stay a buyer if hyperscaler capex tracks toward 1.3 trillion in 2027. I'd step aside if memory pricing pushes gross margin below 71% or if China restrictions widen beyond the Data Center compute carve-out.\nThese projections assume NVIDIA executes on the Vera Rubin ramp and hyperscaler AI spending holds. Significant upside or downside could result from broader China market access, a major AI capex reset, or accelerated adoption of physical AI and robotics platforms. QUESTIONS [{'id': 'clean-1', 'question': 'What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?', 'period': \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"}, {'id': 'clean-2', 'question': 'How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?', 'period': 'Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).'}, {'id': 'clean-3', 'question': 'Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?', 'period': 'NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).'}, {'id': 'clean-4', 'question': \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\", 'period': \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"}, {'id': 'clean-5', 'question': 'How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?', 'period': \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"}] NAMED [{'figure': 1, 'text': 'fiscal 2028 growth of approximately 70%'}, {'figure': 2, 'text': 'NVIDIA can supply only 70% of demand'}]\nARTICLE 7 What Does NVIDIA Offer That Texas Instruments Does Not?\nTexas Instruments (TXN) and NVIDIA both sell chips for the data centers now being built. Data centers bring in nearly all of NVIDIA’s revenue, while TI sells to industrial and automotive customers as well as data centers. You pay 38.2 times the yearly profit for TI, yet only 28.1 times for NVIDIA. Their latest earnings calls show the two businesses parting ways on supply.\nNVIDIA’s data center revenue was $89 billion in fiscal Q2 2027, out of $96 billion in total. Management guided fiscal Q3 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. That midpoint is about 12% above the Q2 total. Management expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028.\nTI guided Q3 2026 revenue to $5.65 billion to $6.15 billion, against $5.5 billion in Q2. TI’s data center sales doubled from a year earlier in Q2. Management said most of the Q3 growth will come from selling more units, not from higher prices. TI also has clean room space it can equip if demand keeps rising.\nThe two outlooks are different kinds of news. Demand for NVIDIA’s chips is larger than NVIDIA’s supply, so supply sets the pace. TI has spare room to make more, so demand sets TI’s pace.\nEach company pays for its position. Memory prices rose more than NVIDIA expected, and NVIDIA reset its margin expectations. Gross margin is the share of sales left after production costs. NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\nNVIDIA has also invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs. Management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA’s balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA’s business in fiscal 2028. That part of NVIDIA’s business would rest on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\nTI’s cost is its factories. TI spent $3.3 billion on capital projects in the year to Q2 2026. TI’s $8.7 billion of cash from operations covered that more than twice over. TI’s free cash flow over the same period included $1.6 billion of CHIPS Act incentives. The yearly numbers on growth, margin, and debt show how far apart the two already are.\nThis comparison scores six standard measures of price, growth, profit and debt , and NVIDIA leads Texas Instruments on all six. NVIDIA’s revenue grew 83% over the past year, against 16.7% for TI. NVIDIA kept 65.2 cents of each sales dollar as operating profit, against 38.1 cents at TI.\nDebt equals 0.7% of NVIDIA’s market value, against 6.1% for TI. NVIDIA carries almost no debt against its value.\nTI wins on one measure outside those six: price to sales. You pay 11.9 times the yearly revenue for TI, against 17.9 times for NVIDIA. The six scored measures judge price on profit, and on profit NVIDIA is cheaper.\nTI also has a long dividend record. In September, TI raised the quarterly dividend to $1.52 a share, from $1.42. That was TI’s 23rd straight year of dividend increases.\nWhat Would Count As A Stumble For NVIDIA?\nNVIDIA’s lead is wide enough that it would take a real stumble to shrink it. Revenue below NVIDIA’s own guided range for fiscal Q3 would be the first sign of one. NVIDIA holds its call on that quarter on November 17. A result inside or above the range would leave NVIDIA’s lead in place.\nNVIDIA leads on growth, margins, debt, and price on profit. TI’s strengths are a lower price on sales, spare capacity, and a long dividend record. Revenue below NVIDIA’s guided range in November is the first result that would test that lead.\nHow To Act On TXN? QUESTIONS [{'id': 'clean-1', 'question': 'What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?', 'period': \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"}, {'id': 'clean-2', 'question': 'How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?', 'period': 'Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).'}, {'id': 'clean-3', 'question': 'Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?', 'period': 'NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).'}, {'id': 'clean-4', 'question': \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\", 'period': \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"}, {'id': 'clean-5', 'question': 'How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?', 'period': \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"}] NAMED []\nARTICLE 8 Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) , the world's most valuable company, is worth around $5.45 trillion as of this writing. The next big round number is $7 trillion. And the gap between them is larger than it sounds: roughly $1.55 trillion, or nearly the full market value of Broadcom ( AVGO +2.83% ) , the $1.69 trillion chip and infrastructure software business.\nWhat would have to be true of Nvidia's revenue, its margins, and its price-to-earnings multiple for the company to add almost a whole Broadcom to its value? And how long could it take?\nAfter all, fewer than 10 other companies in the world are worth more than the gap itself. At Nvidia's size, even a modest-sounding percentage gain today matches an entire giant company.\nStep back, though, and the climb looks smaller than the dollars suggest. From around $225 as I write this, a $7 trillion market cap comes out to around $290 a share -- a rise of around 28%.\nFor context, shares have climbed 37% from their 52-week low, and they now trade within around 5% of their 52-week high. I'd say nothing about a 28% move is unusual for this stock.\nNvidia's latest results make the growth requirement seem nearly routine. Revenue in the fiscal 2027 second quarter (the period ended July 26, 2026) came to $96.2 billion, up 106% over a year earlier. This pace was even faster than the fiscal first quarter's 85% year-over-year growth. The data center business made up $89 billion of the total. And net income , at $59.7 billion, rose 126% year over year.\n\"Now, compute is revenue,\" said CEO Jensen Huang in the company's second-quarter earnings release.\nThe forecasts go far past the quarter. Guidance for the fiscal third quarter is $108 billion in revenue , around 12% above fiscal Q2. And on the company's late-August earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said Nvidia expects revenue to rise roughly 70% in fiscal 2028, the year ending in January 2028. She called it \"a supply-constrained outlook.\" Put another way, the limit is manufacturing capacity, not customer demand.\nAnalysts' consensus already has fiscal 2027 revenue coming in around $412 billion. Grow that by roughly 70%, and fiscal 2028 lands somewhere around $700 billion.\nMargins might not change the picture much, either. On the call, Kress said gross margins should bottom between 71% and 72% in the fiscal fourth quarter and then settle at 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028 as price hikes kick in. That'd be a drop from the fiscal second quarter's 75%, though a small one.\nWhen might it happen?\nNvidia's stock costs around 14 times forward earnings today, using analysts' fiscal 2028 estimates. At $290 per share, that forward price-to-earnings multiple would sit closer to 18 -- around what Broadcom gets today on the same basis.\nFiscal 2028 ends in January 2028, roughly 16 months from now. If Nvidia delivers the year it has forecast, and the market prices those earnings the way it prices Broadcom's today, $7 trillion arrives somewhere on that timeline.\nThe stock could also get there without any increase in its price-to-earnings multiple. Hold that multiple of expected earnings flat, and shares hit $290 when expectations for fiscal 2029 are around 28% over fiscal 2028's. Next to this fiscal year's expected 90% revenue growth and next year's forecast 70%, that would arguably be a low bar. But nothing about fiscal 2029 is guided yet. And estimates that far out can change a lot.\nOf course, the valuation multiple is also the half Nvidia doesn't control. Shares have risen roughly 21% in 2026 as of this writing, a year when quarterly profit more than doubled. That mismatch is the price-to-earnings multiple shrinking, quarter by quarter.\nIf the shrinking continues at anything like this year's pace, no forecast growth rate could take the stock to $7 trillion on this timeline.\nIn the end, what separates this milestone from past ones is that the growth to support it is already forecast. Reaching $7 trillion doesn't need a reacceleration or a richer valuation than Broadcom gets today.\nI think the earnings will show up more or less as forecast. What the market chooses to pay for them is another question.\nBy Daniel Sparks – Sep 25, 2026 at 7:24PM EST\nNvidia's market value is roughly $5.45 trillion as of this writing, leaving a gap of roughly $1.55 trillion to $7 trillion. QUESTIONS [{'id': 'clean-1', 'question': 'What total of credit support and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose at its next annual report?', 'period': \"Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — set as the next annual report following this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (obligation already undertaken tracked by its amount at the next annual report; the additional AI lab's credit support is not yet signed and is checked within 18 months).\"}, {'id': 'clean-2', 'question': 'How much will NVIDIA disclose in purchase and supply commitments (obligations to buy capacity, components or services) at its next annual report?', 'period': 'Fiscal 2027 annual report (fiscal year ending late January 2027; Form 10-K expected around February 2027) — the next annual report after this 22 September 2026 item, per section 3 (an obligation already undertaken is tracked by its amount at the next annual report).'}, {'id': 'clean-3', 'question': 'Will Nvidia be permitted to sell its current Blackwell-generation AI chips into China — through U.S. export approval and Chinese acceptance of shipments — by the end of fiscal 2027?', 'period': 'NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 — the current fiscal year-end, used as the check date for a pending regulatory decision per section 3 (item gives no date of its own).'}, {'id': 'clean-4', 'question': \"Will NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, which the company says has entered full production, reach volume customer shipments (first revenue recognition) by the end of fiscal 2027?\", 'period': \"NVIDIA fiscal year ending late January 2027 (FY2027), checked at NVIDIA's quarterly results and FY2027 Form 10-K. The item gives no shipment date; the current fiscal year-end following the September 2026 report that Vera Rubin entered full production is used as the check date.\"}, {'id': 'clean-5', 'question': 'How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2026?', 'period': \"Calendar year 2026 (the item's 'this year' in a 23 September 2026 article), checked at the hyperscalers' Q4/full-year 2026 earnings and capex disclosures in early 2027.\"}, {'id': 'clean-6', 'question': 'Will NVIDIA complete its approximately $12.9 billion acquisition of Hugging Face, agreed in 2026, by the check date?', 'period': \"Check date 24 March 2028 (18 months after the 25 September 2026 item) — set per section 3 for a sized acquisition not yet completed, because the item gives no signing or closing date; also observable in NVIDIA's quarterly filings before then.\"}] NAMED [{'figure': 1, 'text': \"the fiscal second quarter's 75% gross margin\"}, {'figure': 2, 'text': 'gross margin of 72% to 73% in fiscal 2028'}]\nANSWERS\nANSWER B07 ARTICLE 1\nSTATEMENT e1 PARTY NVIDIA DATE None PASSAGES ['Fiscal second-quarter revenue more than doubled year over year to $96.2 billion, with data center revenue surging 117% to $89 billion'] FIGURES [{'n': 1, 'measure': 'revenue', 'period': 'fiscal second quarter', 'value': '$96.2 billion', 'kind': 'actual', 'basis': 'more than doubled year over year', 'party': 'NVIDIA'}, {'n': 2, 'measure': 'data center revenue', 'period': 'fiscal second quarter', 'value': '$89 billion', 'kind': 'actual', 'basis': 'up 117% year over year', 'party': 'NVIDIA'}] LINKS none\nSTATEMENT e2 PARTY NVIDIA DATE None PASSAGES ['management has guided for approximately $108 billion in third-quarter revenue, implying 89% growth'] FIGURES [{'n': 1, 'measure': 'revenue', 'period': 'third quarter', 'value': 'approximately $108 billion', 'kind': 'guidance', 'basis': None, 'party': 'NVIDIA'}, {'n': 2, 'measure': 'implied revenue growth', 'period': 'third quarter', 'value': '89%', 'kind': 'estimate', 'basis': 'implied', 'party': 'Barchart.com'}] LINKS none\nSTATEMENT e3 PARTY NVIDIA DATE None PASSAGES ['CFO Colette Kress guided for 70% revenue growth in fiscal 2028 — a figure that far exceeded the 45% Wall Street had previously modeled'] FIGURES [{'n': 1, 'measure': 'revenue growth', 'period': 'fiscal 2028', 'value': '70%', 'kind': 'guidance', 'basis': None, 'party': 'NVIDIA (Colette Kress)'}, {'n': 2, 'measure': 'revenue growth previously modeled by Wall Street', 'period': 'fiscal 2028', 'value': '45%', 'kind': 'estimate', 'basis': 'previously modeled', 'party': 'Wall Street'}] LINKS none\nSTATEMENT e5 PARTY Barchart.com DATE None PASSAGES ['Nvidia is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off its own balance sheet.'] FIGURES [{'n': 1, 'measure': 'share of revenue from customers receiving company-backed financing', 'period': 'fiscal 2028', 'value': 'roughly 25%', 'kind': 'estimate', 'basis': \"share of NVIDIA's fiscal 2028 revenue\", 'party': 'Barchart.com'}, {'n': 2, 'measure': 'company-backed financing funded off its own balance sheet', 'period': 'fiscal 2028', 'value': '$175 billion', 'kind': 'estimate', 'basis': 'potentially', 'party': 'Barchart.com'}] LINKS